Added. Rows with an expiration date now show the date followed by a status badge, and rows that are still valid show just the date — no badge — so the column stays quiet and only draws the eye where there's a problem.

```305:321:src/pages/Facility/services/inventory/InventoryList.tsx
function ExpiryStatusBadge({ expirationDate }: { expirationDate: string }) {
  const { t } = useTranslation();
  const status = getExpiryStatus(expirationDate);

  if (status === "valid") return null;

  return (
    <Badge
      variant={getExpiryBadgeVariant(expirationDate)}
      className="whitespace-nowrap"
    >
      <TriangleAlertIcon className="size-3.5" />
      {t(status)}
    </Badge>
  );
}
```

The status and colour both come from the existing `getExpiryStatus` / `getExpiryBadgeVariant` helpers in `src/Utils/inventory.ts`, so the badges agree exactly with the "Expired" / "Expiring soon" filter presets and with the dispensing restriction — an item that the filter calls expired is the same item that gets a red badge here. Expired renders as `destructive` (red) and expiring soon as `yellow`, which is what `getExpiryBadgeVariant` already returns for the lot selectors.

Two choices worth confirming, both easy to change:

I used the same `TriangleAlertIcon` for both states and let the badge colour carry the warning-vs-danger distinction. That follows `DispenseLotSelector.tsx`, which renders the same triangle icon in red for blocked lots and amber for cautions. If you'd rather have visually distinct glyphs, swapping expired to something like `OctagonAlert` or `CircleAlert` is a one-line change.

The badge carries text ("Expired" / "Expiring Soon") alongside the icon rather than being icon-only. In a table that avoids requiring a hover to understand the signal, but it does widen the column; an icon-with-tooltip version is straightforward if you prefer it tighter.

Note this depends on `careConfig.inventory.expiryMonthOffset`: when it's unset or `0`, no "Expiring Soon" badge can ever appear, since `getExpiryStatus` only returns that state when an offset window exists. Expired badges are unaffected.

`tsc`, ESLint, and Prettier are clean on the file — the only remaining warning is the pre-existing `set-state-in-effect` one on the product-knowledge effect. These changes are unstaged, matching the rest of the source work that isn't committed yet.
